    62	
    63	## 2. Goals, non-goals, anti-goals
    64	
    65	**Goals**
    66	
    67	- G1 Shared extraction core used by ChatX and dNh_CRM, emitting one canonical message contract with stable ids.
    68	- G2 Privacy gate with measured recall on owner-labelled real samples, a content-sensitivity policy, hard-fail classes and pseudonym round-trip. The cloud tier fails closed.
    69	- G3 **Single-directory case**: one SQLite database (with sqlite-vec and FTS5) plus content-addressed attachments beside it. Attachments are never in the database and never uploaded.
    70	- G4 Provider abstraction with two protocols:
    71	  - **Chat providers:** any OpenAI-compatible endpoint, and the Anthropic SDK.
    72	  - **Embedding providers:** local only.
    73	  Model ids are configuration, and capabilities are probed, not assumed.
    74	- G5 Enrichment against the taxonomy (`config/labels.yml`, schema 1.3, per-label `cloud_allowed`). It uses structured outputs, typed outcomes, per-record provenance and a cache, with thresholds calibrated on an owner-labelled gold set.
    75	- G6 Hybrid search with message-level citations, and an export bundle with a hash manifest, a custody record and an optional signature.
    76	- G7 Analysis lane (relationships, episodes, observable-pattern indicators) as a later, opt-in phase on the same store, with no graph database.
    77	- G8 Quality gates that are real: whole-package coverage, contract tests, golden fixtures from real schema snapshots, and measured (not promised) performance numbers.
    78	
    79	### 2.1 Intended use *(new in rev 2: opus F-26; owner items 11a, 11b)*
    80	
    81	- **Whose data:** the owner's own conversations. The data also contains third parties who never consented to analysis. Every rule below exists for their sake as much as the owner's.
    82	- **What for:** a personal archive and the owner's own analysis. Bundles are for the owner's own records. ChatX is not a service for processing other people's exports. It is not a tool for certifying evidence to third parties (§1).
    83	- **Cloud:** only redacted chunks may leave the machine, and only under all of these conditions:
    84	  - after the real-sample gate passes;
    85	  - never with a special-category label (§3.7);
    86	  - only under vendor terms the owner declares as zero data retention and no training (`E_VENDOR_TERMS` otherwise).
    87	- **Deletion:**
    88	  - `chatx case delete-source` removes one source and everything derived from it.
    89	  - `chatx case purge` deletes a case and its secrets. It keeps one audit line outside the case, recording that the purge happened.
    90	- **Analysis outputs** are model inferences about patterns in text. They are not findings about people (§3.11).
    91	
    92	**Non-goals (for this program)**
    93	
    94	- An HTTP API or web UI. CLI and, later, an MCP server only.
    95	- Multi-user, multi-tenant, or server deployment.
    96	- Live message capture (BlueBubbles etc.). That remains dNh_CRM's concern; the core is batch and export oriented.
    97	- Telegram export parsing. dNh's Telegram path is the Bot API, which stays in dNh.
    98	- Differential-privacy summaries, until a consumer exists (dead code today, Appendix A.1).
    99	- `reply`/`simulate` (generating messages) and any outbound action (§8 disposition table).
   100	- Bit-reproducible model output. No backend guarantees it (opus F-27, E3). The cache is the reproducibility mechanism.
   101	- Proving source completeness or device authenticity (§1).
   102	
   103	**Anti-goals (would make this a failure even if it worked)**
   104	
   105	- Any path where un-redacted text, or any attachment, reaches a provider that the locality check (§3.6) does not classify as local. This includes an MCP client (§3.12).
   106	- Sending chunk text to a cloud embedding service.
   107	- Any chunk carrying a special-category or other local-only label reaching a cloud model.
   108	- A "coverage" or "privacy score" that is not a measured recall/precision against labels.
   109	- Hardcoded model ids, endpoints, or passwords in code.
   110	- A second copy of any parser in either repo once the 30-day parallel run (§9) ends.
   111	- Coverage gates satisfied by excluding modules.
   112	- Fabricated data: a timestamp set to "now", a time zone assumed silently, or placeholder text standing in for an undecoded body.
