# Cross-project cost-first delegation

Applies to Codex, Claude Code, Grok, and Antigravity (AGY), across all projects.

## Mandatory packet routing

For every non-trivial task or Task Packet, inspect governing instructions first, decompose the work, and delegate suitable bounded subtasks when authorized. Delegation is the default, not an optional suggestion. Use the cheapest available qualified model and the lowest sufficient supported reasoning effort that meet the same correctness, completeness, security, and validation requirements. Use deterministic local tools for shell, inventory, hashes, schemas, and other mechanical operations; do not spend a model call merely to execute a deterministic check.

Before dispatch, record a concise routing plan: subtask, owner, selected model/effort, capability and cost basis, acceptance checks, and applicable call/retry budget. Do not automatically assign every child the supervisor's model. For routine work start with one bounded worker; add parallel workers only for independent work with clear ownership and justified benefit. Avoid redundant agents and recursive delegation unless the task warrants them and authority permits them.

## Qualification and runner controls

Use documented per-agent or per-invocation model selectors and supported effort controls. Qualify the actual client/account availability and suitability for the subtask. Prefer fresh or bounded-history children when full-history inheritance prevents cheaper model selection. A selector or config declaration does not prove actual execution identity. Report requested versus observed model/effort separately when runtime receipts exist; otherwise observed identity is UNKNOWN.

Compare current costs or account billing conditions where available, including context overhead and likely rework; there is no permanent cross-provider price ranking. If cost is unproven, label it UNKNOWN and state the selection basis. Never invent model IDs, effort values, availability, prices, or savings. If a runner cannot select child models or lacks delegation, report the limitation rather than pretending the policy was enforced. Do not introduce another runner/provider or billable route solely to satisfy delegation without authority.

## Worker scope and quality

Each child receives the relevant packet and repo instructions, explicit file/responsibility ownership, allowed tools and mutation scope, deliverable, acceptance checks, and stop conditions. Tell editing workers that they share the workspace, must preserve others' edits, and must not revert unrelated work. Send only the context needed for the bounded task; preserve evidence references and keep secrets out of prompts.

Offload bounded research, code tracing, routine implementation, documentation, and focused validation when the selected model is adequate. Keep architecture decisions, security/replay/concurrency judgment, unresolved ambiguity, cross-cutting synthesis, escalation, and final acceptance with a sufficiently capable supervisor or explicitly qualified specialist. A cheaper model is acceptable only if it meets the required quality bar; do not reduce scope, evidence, tests, or output quality to save cost.

The supervisor inspects delegated results and runs or verifies relevant acceptance checks before relying on them. Worker claims and successful tool exits are not proof of correctness. Preserve PASS, FAIL, NOT_RUN, UNKNOWN, and other required gate states literally. Delegated review cannot replace a required independent audit unless its independence and route satisfy the governing audit contract.

## Escalation and exceptions

On insufficient quality, uncertainty, failed validation, or demonstrated complexity/risk, the child stops and returns evidence to the supervisor. The supervisor narrows the unresolved slice and assigns a more capable qualified model only within existing authority and remaining budgets. Record the reason and bound the attempt. No hidden upgrade, retry, fallback, provider substitution, or indefinite cheap-model repair loop.

Explicit user and active repository/Task Packet model, runner, provider, billing, approval, same-route, no-fallback, retry, and auditor restrictions prevail. This policy never relaxes those gates. If delegation is prohibited, unavailable, or no suitable subtask exists, state the exact exception and continue only with independently authorized work; stop the affected work when its contract cannot be satisfied. Never manufacture a child call to meet a quota.

At completion, summarize delegated subtasks, requested and observed models/efforts, validation evidence, escalations, and exceptions. Do not claim universal enforcement, guaranteed equal quality, or measured savings without evidence.
