   232	## T18 Packaging & uv workspace — codex F21, F22 · grok F16 · opus F-10 · chatgpt F29, F30, F36
   233	
   234	Change (decided), §3.2, §3.3:
   235	- dNh consumes a **built wheel pinned by version and hash** (from a git tag / local index), not an editable path dependency; the editable path dep is for a dNh development branch only. "dNh pins the version" now means bytes, not a string.
   236	- Package data via `importlib.resources`; no `Path(__file__).parents[n]`. The core's dependency set is listed in the design (pydantic, phonenumbers) and a test asserts its import graph contains no network/LLM/storage modules.
   237	- **uv syntax** *(answered 2026-10-21 by **E8**; was RT-8)*: from outside the core's workspace, dNh's development branch can use `{ path = "<core dir>", editable = true }`, a plain path dependency that does not apply the other workspace's lock or sources, or `{ workspace = "<workspace root>" }`, which resolves the member by name. Release builds use the pinned wheel above. P0 proves both from clean clones.
   238	- *(opus F-10)* Covered by the built-wheel bullet above: dNh CI installs a pinned version, never the editable path.
   239	- *(chatgpt F29)* Release contract: core releases are git tags `core-vX.Y.Z` with a wheel and its sha256 built in CI from a clean tree; dNh's lock records that hash. Before tagging, the core's CI runs dNh's adapter tests against the new wheel.
   240	- *(chatgpt F30)* A missing schema resource is an error (`E_SCHEMA_MISSING`), never a skip; v1's validator returns `True` when the schema file is absent. CI installs the built wheel into a fresh venv outside the checkout and runs the CLI smoke tests there.
   241	- *(chatgpt F36)* Model artifacts are pinned by repository, revision and file sha256, alongside the dependency hashes; third-party notices ship with both packages. Licence and ownership (owner item 13, 2026-10-21): the core is MIT-licensed, like ChatX, and owned by the owner (DDD-Enterprises). Proprietary dNh may depend on it.
   242	
   276	## T22 Operations, intent regression & CI gates — codex F26, F27 · agy F-25 · opus F-30 · chatgpt F34, F35
   277	
   278	Change (decided), §3.12, §6 P0, new §8 disposition table:
   279	- CLI gains `chatx case resume | repair | delete-source`, `chatx import … --resume`, `chatx index build --incremental`.
   280	- Disposition of old requirements: `backfill` → `import --resume`; `index update` → `index build --incremental`; `simulate`, `reply` → dropped (no consumer, reason stated); NFR "1M-message corpus" → tracked measurement at P5 (T13).
   281	- P0 scope explicitly includes **rewriting** `.github/workflows/*` and `config/arch-gates.yml`; the old gate file is not carried.
   282	- *(opus F-30)* Gate refusal moves from exit 2, which is Click/Typer's usage-error code, to exit 3 (decided). The exit-code table is documented in `docs/reference/errors.md` next to the RFC 9457 problem types. **E7** (2026-10-21) confirms Click's `UsageError.exit_code` is 2; that Typer inherits it is inferred.
   283	- *(chatgpt F34)* §3.12 gains a behaviour table. Exit codes: 0 success, 1 error, 2 usage (Click), 3 gate refused, 4 partial (counts printed and recorded). Per command it states what a partial or interrupted run leaves (watermark, `status=partial`) and what `delete-source` and `case purge` remove and keep. Every input record ends in exactly one disposition, and the report's counts sum to the input count (asserted). A case directory is self-contained, with paths stored relative to the case root, so it can be moved.
